  "code": "STrans",
  "cost": 0,
  "interpretation": "<table><thead><tr><th><div>Transferrin Level</div></th><th><div>Interpretation</div></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><div>Normal Range (typically 200-360 mg/dL)</div></td><td><div>Indicates normal levels of transferrin in the blood</div></td></tr><tr><td><div>Increased Levels</div></td><td><div>High transferrin levels may be seen in conditions such as iron deficiency anemia or chronic inflammatory disorders. It can also be a response to certain medications or acute liver damage. Further evaluation is necessary to determine the underlying cause.</div></td></tr><tr><td><div>Decreased Levels</div></td><td><div>Low transferrin levels may suggest iron overload disorders such as hereditary hemochromatosis or chronic liver disease. It can also be seen in malnutrition, protein deficiency, or chronic kidney disease. Additional testing is required to identify the underlying cause.</div></td></tr></tbody></table>",
